'''''Salix eriocephala''''', known as '''heart-leaved willow''' or '''Missouri River willow''', is a species of [[Salix|willow]] native to a large portion of the temperate United States and Canada.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org/species/salix/eriocephala/|title=''Salix eriocephala'' (heart-leaved willow): Go Botany|website=gobotany.nativeplanttrust.org|access-date=17 November 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.minnesotawildflowers.info/shrub/missouri-river-willow|title=''Salix eriocephala'' (Missouri River Willow): Minnesota Wildflowers|website=www.minnesotawildflowers.info|access-date=17 November 2020}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://plants.usda.gov/plant-profile?symbol=SAER|title=Plants Profile for Salix eriocephala (Missouri River willow)|website=plants.usda.gov|access-date=17 November 2020}}</ref>

It is usually found as a narrow shrub or small tree with multiple trunks growing to a height of {{cvt|20|ft}}. It has dark gray, scaly bark with thick lance-shaped leaves that are hairy underneath. The silky catkins appear before the leaves in early spring.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.wildflower.org/plants/result.php?id_plant=saer|title=Lady Bird Johnson Wildflower Center - The University of Texas at Austin|website=www.wildflower.org|access-date=17 November 2020}}</ref>

